topics.description:
**Web Components** are a set of browser standards to build **reusable, encapsulated** elements without a framework.

### The three technologies
1. **Custom Elements** - define new tags (`customElements.define('my-tag', Class)`). The name **must contain a hyphen**.
2. **Shadow DOM** - a private DOM + CSS scope inside the element. Page styles do not leak in, component styles do not leak out.
3. **HTML Templates & Slots** - reusable markup and content projection.

### Custom element skeleton
```js
class UserBadge extends HTMLElement {
  static observedAttributes = ['name'];
  connectedCallback() {          // added to the page
    this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' }).innerHTML =
      '<style>span{color:tomato}</style><span></span>';
    this.render();
  }
  attributeChangedCallback() { this.render(); }
  render() {
    const s = this.shadowRoot && this.shadowRoot.querySelector('span');
    if (s) s.textContent = 'Hi ' + this.getAttribute('name');
  }
}
customElements.define('user-badge', UserBadge);
```

### Lifecycle callbacks
`constructor`, `connectedCallback`, `disconnectedCallback`, `attributeChangedCallback`, `adoptedCallback`.

### Using it
`<user-badge name="Asha"></user-badge>` - works in plain HTML, React, Vue and Angular.

### Pros and cons
- ✅ Framework independent, encapsulated, standard.
- ⚠️ Styling from outside needs CSS variables or `::part()`; server rendering and forms need extra work.

